# Local overrides, layered on top of .env. Copy to .env.local and adjust.
#
#   make env-local        (or: copy /Y .env.example.local .env.local)
#
# .env.local is git-ignored and is read BEFORE .env, so anything set here wins.
# A real environment variable set by the platform or by compose wins over both,
# and the file is not read at all when APP_ENV=production — so this is for local
# development only and can never reach a deployment.
#
# Its purpose is to let one machine run against a local mail sink while .env
# keeps the credentials a deployment needs. Nothing else has to be duplicated
# here: only the values that must differ locally.

# --- Mail: point the transport at Mailpit ----------------------------------
# `make up-tools` starts Mailpit; it catches every message instead of sending
# it, so the mail path can be exercised without a provider account and without
# authorising an IP address. Read what it caught at http://localhost:8025, or
# with `make mail`.
#
# The host differs by how the API runs, and that is the one thing to get right:
#   - API on the host (`make run`):        SMTP_HOST=localhost
#   - API in a container (`make up-tools`): SMTP_HOST=mailpit
# The dev compose overlay already sets the container value, so this file is
# only needed for the host case.
SMTP_HOST=localhost
SMTP_PORT=1025

# Mailpit accepts any sender and needs no credentials. Clearing them here stops
# a provider credential from being offered to a server that does not want one.
SMTP_USERNAME=
SMTP_PASSWORD=

# Any From address is accepted by Mailpit, so SMTP_FROM is deliberately left to
# .env rather than repeated here.

# --- What does NOT belong here ---------------------------------------------
# Compose does not read this file. It interpolates ${VAR} from .env and from the
# shell, so anything compose itself needs — DB_PORT and REDIS_PORT, which decide
# the published host ports of the dev overlay — must be set in .env. Putting them
# here would have no effect on the containers and would look like it should.
#
# This file is for the Go process: values the API reads directly, where a local
# value has to win over the one .env carries for deployment.
